<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boris Pasternak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boris Pasternak was a Russian poet, novelist and translator, one of the major lyric poets of the twentieth century. His novel Doctor Zhivago, rejected by Soviet publishers, appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1957 and became an international success. When he was awarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1958, an official campaign of vilification forced him to decline it, and he died in disgrace two years later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boris-pasternak.jpg" alt="Boris Pasternak" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Владимир Савостьянов / ТАСС · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boris_Pasternak_1958_photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Boris Leonidovich Pasternak was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 10 February 1890 (29 January in the old style), into a wealthy, assimilated Jewish family. His father, Leonid Pasternak, was a post-Impressionist painter who taught at the Moscow School of Painting, Sculpture, and Architecture; his mother, Rosa Kaufman, was a concert pianist. The family home was visited by Sergei Rachmaninoff, Rainer Maria Rilke and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a>.</p><p>Pasternak first studied music at the Moscow Conservatory and composed, and he later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to study philosophy. In 1912 he met Ida Wissotzkaya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a>. Her family, troubled by his poor prospects, persuaded her to refuse his proposal, and he told of the rejection in the poem Marburg, written in 1917.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Pasternak's first book of poems, My Sister, Life, was composed in 1917 and published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1922, and it soon became an important collection in the Russian language. He also became a literary translator, rendering plays by Goethe, Schiller, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-calderon-de-la-barca">Pedro Calderón de la Barca</a> and Shakespeare. His translation of the first part of Faust drew an attack in Novy Mir in August 1950, which accused him of distorting Goethe's meanings.</p><p>In 1934 Osip Mandelstam recited his epigram on Stalin to him; Mandelstam was arrested that May, and Pasternak appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-bukharin">Nikolai Bukharin</a> on his behalf. In 1937 he refused to sign a writers' statement supporting death sentences in the trial of Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-tukhachevsky">Mikhail Tukhachevsky</a> and others. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he served as a fire warden in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and in 1943 visited soldiers at the front.</p><p>Doctor Zhivago, which contains passages from the 1910s and 1920s, was completed in 1955. Novy Mir refused it in 1956 for its rejection of socialist realism, and censors objected to its criticism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a>, collectivisation, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>. It was first published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1957. Pasternak was awarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1958 and was forced to decline it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pasternak died of lung cancer at his dacha in Peredelkino on 30 May 1960. Despite a small notice in the Literary Gazette, handwritten notices of the funeral were posted through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> subway, and thousands of admirers attended despite surveillance. His companion Olga Ivinskaya and her daughter were arrested after his death and accused of dealing with Western publishers; she served four years of an eight-year sentence.</p><p>The Soviet state kept criticising him until the 1980s. His son Yevgeny accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> on his behalf in 1989, and the house-museum in Peredelkino was founded in 1986. Doctor Zhivago has been part of the main Russian school curriculum since 2003, and David Lean's film of 1965 was unavailable in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> until perestroika.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boris Pasternak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Soviet culture combined official socialist realism with world-class achievement in music, ballet, film and literature, from Sergei Prokofiev, Dmitri Shostakovich and Sergei Eisenstein to <b>Boris Pasternak</b> and Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn, whose account of the Gulag shaped Western views of the system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Peredelkino, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1935" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Boris Pasternak"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
